1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,940
A million evaluations to price an option on a hundred stocks: an average over a hundred inputs.

2
00:00:07,140 --> 00:00:09,740
Spread them as a grid; that feels thorough.

3
00:00:09,940 --> 00:00:12,260
How many values does each input get?

4
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:14,850
One.

5
00:00:15,050 --> 00:00:17,670
The whole grid is one point: the centre.

6
00:00:17,870 --> 00:00:21,490
Two values per input would take two to the hundredth points.

7
00:00:22,533 --> 00:00:29,943
Since Metropolis and Ulam in 1949, the standard tool has been random points, like this one.

8
00:00:30,443 --> 00:00:33,283
How far off would a million random points land?

9
00:00:33,933 --> 00:00:35,413
Start with two inputs.

10
00:00:35,613 --> 00:00:42,643
Our function is one inside this quarter disc, zero outside: its average is exactly one half.

11
00:00:43,233 --> 00:00:47,613
A grid of a hundred points puts fifty-two inside: two points off.

12
00:00:48,066 --> 00:00:52,466
A hundred random darts put forty-four inside: six points off.

13
00:00:52,716 --> 00:00:54,966
In two dimensions, the grid wins.

14
00:00:55,433 --> 00:00:58,533
Now give each a million points, and add inputs.

15
00:00:58,733 --> 00:01:02,823
The disc becomes a ball, grown to cover exactly half the box.

16
00:01:03,266 --> 00:01:09,546
Per axis, the grid gets a thousand values in two dimensions, then a hundred, ten, three.

17
00:01:10,133 --> 00:01:13,863
From twenty dimensions on, just the centre, inside the ball.

18
00:01:14,013 --> 00:01:16,493
So the grid's estimate is the whole box.

19
00:01:16,793 --> 00:01:18,113
Fifty points off.

20
00:01:19,366 --> 00:01:23,596
Even two values on nineteen axes: eighteen points off.

21
00:01:23,896 --> 00:01:25,986
The darts stay under a tenth of a point.

22
00:01:27,400 --> 00:01:31,050
Maybe darts win by giving every axis more values.

23
00:01:31,850 --> 00:01:41,270
A million points on the diagonal give every axis a million values, and still miss by eight points: their coordinates move in lockstep.

24
00:01:42,033 --> 00:01:45,793
A dart's coordinates are drawn independently and uniformly.

25
00:01:46,093 --> 00:01:50,773
So it lands inside with a chance equal to the answer, in any dimension.

26
00:01:51,173 --> 00:01:52,923
Each dart is a coin flip.

27
00:01:54,133 --> 00:01:55,813
A flip is zero or one.

28
00:01:55,963 --> 00:02:00,433
Its average is a half, so every flip misses by exactly a half.

29
00:02:01,033 --> 00:02:04,513
Repeat the hundred-dart experiment three hundred times.

30
00:02:04,713 --> 00:02:07,063
In both, the estimates spread this wide.

31
00:02:08,766 --> 00:02:12,846
Those misses fall up or down at random, so they partly cancel.

32
00:02:13,146 --> 00:02:16,876
Sixteen darts typically stray four halves, not sixteen.

33
00:02:17,176 --> 00:02:21,766
What adds is their squares: sixteen of them fill a square four halves wide.

34
00:02:22,166 --> 00:02:24,426
N darts stray about root N halves.

35
00:02:24,726 --> 00:02:28,666
Divide by N, and the typical miss is a half over root N.

36
00:02:29,466 --> 00:02:31,876
For a hundred darts: five points.

37
00:02:32,376 --> 00:02:33,946
We measured four point eight.

38
00:02:34,246 --> 00:02:39,166
Any other function swaps the half for its own spread, sigma; there's still no d.

39
00:02:39,633 --> 00:02:42,913
Back to our ball: a million darts, any dimension.

40
00:02:44,413 --> 00:02:47,963
One half over a thousand: five hundredths of a point.

41
00:02:49,300 --> 00:02:57,590
In 2012, Bergstra and Bengio found random search tuned neural networks as well as grid search in a fraction of the time.

42
00:02:58,040 --> 00:03:08,540
Few settings really matter; with one, the search is one-dimensional: nine grid trials test three values of it; nine random trials test nine.

43
00:03:09,300 --> 00:03:12,200
Darts have a blind spot: tiny answers.

44
00:03:12,400 --> 00:03:19,480
Keep the ball's radius at one, and in thirty dimensions it fills two parts in a hundred trillion of the box.

45
00:03:19,880 --> 00:03:28,440
Below this line, a million darts expect under one hit, so they usually report zero: a tiny miss, but the whole answer.

46
00:03:29,966 --> 00:03:32,296
The darts never counted the axes:

47
00:03:32,696 --> 00:03:35,326
a million coin flips, in any dimension.

